WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.435 align:start size:84% position:16%
- What I do in my book
is rather than say,

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.637
okay, all of these
things that people

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.106
have been canceled for
joking about you can,

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:09.009 align:start size:84% position:16%
I take it 9 million
steps further.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.144
And I say, there's no such thing

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
as something that
you can't joke about.

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:15.015
And yes, I'm including religion.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:16.950
Yes, I'm including death.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.520
Yes, I'm including
terminal illness

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.256
because there's research
that backs this stuff up.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.225
I'm not just saying this.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:28.228
(suspenseful music)

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.871
is the co-host of
the number one show

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:41.041
in late night, "Gutfeld!"
with an exclamation point

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:42.509
as well as author
of the new book.

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.477
"You Can't Joke About That:

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.414 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why Everything Is Funny
Nothing Is Sacred."

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:50.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we're all in this
together, my friend Kat Timpf.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.453
Welcome finally, is this
your first appearance?

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.755
This is it, first time
on "The Rubin Report?"

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.358 align:start size:75% position:25%
- It is.
It is actually.

00:00:58.358 --> 00:00:59.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's been a long
time coming, but yes,

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.695
this is my very first time.
I'm very excited to be here.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.130
- Very first time.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.200 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, first off, technically
am I, is that correct?

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.602
Are you a co-host on "Gutfeld"?

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you're in the
chair basically every day

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.206
and Tyrus is in the other chair

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:14.707
and then there are
two other guests.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:17.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
But are you technically,
did I get that right?

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.778
- Yeah, no, that's right.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.947
Greg will call me that,
so I think it's allowed.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.382
- I told you right
before we started

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.250 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I'm gonna treat
you like Greg treats you

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.952
because I want you
to feel at home here.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:26.986
Is that, is that
all right with you?

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.488
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.057
- Okay, very good.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.559
Let's get, well,
before we get into

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:33.660
some of the politics stuff

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.428
and the stuff around free speech

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
and all the stuff that we're
normally talking about,

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:40.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
you go a bit into
growing up and youth

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.268
and how that sort of frames

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of the beliefs
that you have.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.538 align:start size:88% position:13%
So you wanna do a little
bit of that first?

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:47.974
- Are you talking
about, you know,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I started doing
standup back in,

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.980
you know, when I was growing
up, when I was 22, that's-

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I was even going
back further than that,

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:81% position:19%
growing up in a
Catholic household

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
and some of the sacred cows
there, but we can do that too.

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.288 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, okay.
Yeah, I mean it's like,

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.656
that's the first chapter

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:06.426 align:start position:0%
and then the Catholic household
is the second chapter.

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:07.961
I can get into either one.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:13.066 align:start size:97% position:3%
So for me, I mean this is
the final chapter in my book,

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.969
but for me, comedy
is a religion for me

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.572
because I unfortunately
don't have a religion.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.708
I say unfortunately because
I think I probably would be,

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know I would be happier
if I were religious,

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I just, I can't
really seem to get there.

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:27.747 align:start size:84% position:16%
I hope that it changes
someday for me.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:31.117
But comedy actually does
a lot of the same things

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:33.319
as religion does, right?

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.921
It brings people
closer together.

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:38.491 align:start size:97% position:3%
It can add meaning to certain
experiences in your life,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.161
including the traumatic ones.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.962 align:start size:84% position:16%
It can, you know,
be healing mentally.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.198 align:start size:97% position:3%
It can be healing emotionally
and even physically.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.733
The boost that you
get from laughter

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.469
and your immunity and there's
all these different studies,

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.638 align:start size:88% position:13%
the two things that
it's really missing is,

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:53.740
first of all, there's no
promise of eternal life

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.576
with any joke, no matter how
good it is, unfortunately.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:03:00.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the second thing that's
missing is forgiveness, right?

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:04.584
Because every religion has some
sort of path to forgiveness.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.220 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Catholic church
was confession,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
which I would go and
always be honest,

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.525
even when it was I was
old enough where my sins

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.027
were a little bit
less comfortable

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:17.463
to talk to an older
strange man about.

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you learn, you
read the Bible, right?

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.403
Leviticus is Old Testament.

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.004
So it's like not,
it's not chill,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's like, and the New
Testament comes in,

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.075
is like supposed to
be about forgiveness

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.678
and chill and all, you
know, more easy going.

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:34.414
Leviticus is extremely
fire and brimstone text.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:37.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
But even Leviticus, the
standard is eye for an eye,

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
what we're doing right
now with comedy and speech

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.655
is actually stricter

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.525 align:start size:88% position:13%
and more fire and
brimstone than Leviticus

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:48.928
because I don't think
anybody would tell,

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.363
would be able to
honestly tell you

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:51.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
the worst thing that
they've ever been through

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:54.601 align:start size:97% position:3%
is hearing a joke that
they thought was offensive.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.135
But there's-

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:59.772 align:start size:97% position:3%
- If you watch Jimmy Kimmel,
it can be quite grueling.

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:01.941
- Well see, but there's
more than one person

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:04.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
who would say telling
the wrong joke

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.746
actually is the worst thing
that they've been through

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.314 align:start size:81% position:19%
because they maybe
lost everything

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.882
or lost millions and
millions of dollars

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
or lost their
respect or, you know,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:15.888
ability to come and be a
human in the square, right?

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.423
The public square.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:21.261
So I feel like if the way
that we are treating jokes

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:25.898
is stricter than Leviticus,
we're doing something wrong.

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.900
- What year did you
start doing standup?

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:30.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So I started
doing it in college.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:34.807
I did it a few times, so I
graduated college in 2010.

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:38.711
And then after that,
when I left college,

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:42.682 align:start size:97% position:3%
I moved to LA, it was supposed
to be a short-term move

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:45.318 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then I was going to, I
was gonna, I had a fellowship,

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:46.286
a stipend to live out there.

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:48.021
I had an internship at Fox News

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:50.356
and then I had gotten into
Columbia Journalism School.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.726
So I was gonna use my stipend
for housing until it ran out

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:56.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then live with my college
boyfriend for two weeks.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:58.498
And then we were gonna break
up and then I was gonna go

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:01.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
to Columbia and it
didn't work out that way.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:03.503
It was a very, I'm 21,
so it's a very dumb plan,

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.239
but where I wasn't dumb was
taking out an $80,000 loan

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:07.774
for journalism school.

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.443
So I decided instead
to just stay in LA,

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:11.711
get journalism internships

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.080
and learn skills for
free and wait tables.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.549
But things got really
rough for a while.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.318
The boyfriend broke up with me.

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.921 align:start position:0%
I had to move into this really,
I was living in squalor.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:24.590
I mean, I got fleas and
scabies the same week.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.059
My cat got the fleas,
I got the scabies,

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:27.593 align:start size:81% position:19%
but then the fleas
jumped onto me.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.595
That was the same week
I lost my apartment.

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:30.797
I was running myself ragged.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.865
I write up all about
it in my first chapter.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:37.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
But what I was doing that
made me feel power in my life

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.304
was standup comedy.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.906
Every free moment I had,

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:43.109
I was going to open
mics anywhere I could

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:46.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
and talking about my
dumpster fire life on stage

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:48.748
and making jokes
about these things

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:50.083
that were making me
feel so helpless,

00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:51.884
gave me some power over it

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:55.088 align:start size:84% position:16%
and hearing people
laugh and all of that.

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:59.292
And it just makes me kind of
nervous and terrified and upset

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:00.927
about what we're seeing
happen in our culture

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:03.663
where you can't joke
about sad or serious

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:06.999 align:start size:84% position:16%
or controversial
subjects when really,

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.235
that's the way that
I've found healing

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.437
through every tough time
in my life was by joking

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the stuff you're not
supposed to joke about.

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:14.674
- Do you look back on them?

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:15.842
I think I already
know the answer

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:18.544 align:start size:88% position:13%
and think of those
years, the scabies years

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.713
as the good old days
because I know for me,

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
for those 10 years of
struggling in New York City

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:26.486
as a standup and standing out
in Times Square with tickets

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:28.454
and literally being
broke to the point

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:29.889
that I had a friend who would,

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
who worked in food service
and would deliver me

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:34.761
industrial sized cans of tuna
that I would eat for weeks

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:37.964
and all of that stuff, I now
look back at them kind of like,

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:39.499
oh those were the good old days.

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:42.201
Like, I have everything I
want now, my life is good,

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:45.571
but like there's something
charming about that struggle

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:48.741
once you make it to the
other side, I guess.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:49.942
- Right, exactly.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
It actually is really
strange just how,

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:57.183
like in what a specific way I
was able to achieve my goals.

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:58.351
Because back when that guy

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:00.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I were still
dating in Los Angeles,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.021 align:start size:88% position:13%
we were at his brother's
house and we used to

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:04.090
always watch Red Eye because
it was on at midnight there.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.993
And I was like, I would
be so good on this show.

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:08.461
And his brother was like,

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:12.565
"You're a cashier at Boston
Market", which was true.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:15.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you think in terms of
what's likely to be possible,

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:16.769
I shouldn't have even
gotten on the show,

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:19.172
let alone been the
co-host of the show

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:21.073
that would follow that up.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.442
But it is remarkable.

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:25.178
But man, those times
were absolutely rough.

00:07:25.178 --> 00:07:27.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean the treatment
for scabies,

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:28.347
I dunno if you've ever had it.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:29.549
You have to cover your
entire body in a cream

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just sit there
for like hours on end.

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.120
And the fact that that was
the same time I had to move

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.291
in with this Colombian
family of this bartender

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:41.027
that I was sort of seeing at
my California pizza kitchen job

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:42.562 align:start size:88% position:13%
and all these
conversations about how

00:07:42.562 --> 00:07:44.096
just 'cause I need to
live here doesn't mean

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.265
we're together, together,

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:48.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
just sitting there at
his house, his mom's ...

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.235
My Spanish was never better.

00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:50.670
Which is I think is like

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.705 align:start size:84% position:16%
the one positive thing
of that experience.

00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:55.808
I was very good at
Spanish back then,

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:58.744
but you know, like,
I don't know how

00:07:58.744 --> 00:07:59.712
I would've gotten through it

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.681
if I wasn't able to laugh at it

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:04.750 align:start size:81% position:19%
because if you
laugh at something

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
then you're telling yourself,
oh, it's not that scary.

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:09.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or you're telling that
thing, you're not that scary.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:11.057
And that's the only way I
was able to get through it.

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:14.927
And now, I'm just
worried that that's

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.963 align:start size:88% position:13%
not gonna be possible
for many people anymore.

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:18.364
People are too afraid.

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:19.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Wait, I wanna keep
going with that.

00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:21.100
But you worked at Boston Market

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.869
and California Pizza Kitchen.

00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:25.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
Any other major
chains on your resume?

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:26.739
- Ruby Tuesday.

00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:29.976
Oh, and Ruby Tuesday, with
the salad bar and everything.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:32.645
- Yes. I also worked at a diner

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:35.882
called Four'n Twenty
Diners at Sherman Oaks.

00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:37.817
It was a pie place.

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:39.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was supposed to be
four and twenty blackbirds

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:40.353
baked in a pie.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:41.821
But of course everybody,

00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:42.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of people would
think it was a restaurant

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:43.956
that just made edibles.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:46.425
So I would have to
explain to people

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:49.061 align:start size:88% position:13%
like, no, there's no
weed in our food, sorry.

00:08:49.061 --> 00:08:50.129
- I used to live
in Sherman Oaks,

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:51.631
I don't remember the pie place,

00:08:51.631 --> 00:08:53.866 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that might have been the
edibles. I'm not so sure.

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:56.936 align:start size:97% position:3%
Does this all like, seem
very obvious to you in a way?

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:58.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, you know, obviously
on the show you guys

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:00.439
are talking about the
woke thing constantly

00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:02.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it just has infected
everything, everyone gets it.

00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:06.245 align:start size:88% position:13%
But when you see just
all of these hysterical,

00:09:06.245 --> 00:09:09.215
joyless kids who are so
confused about reality

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:12.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
and have no sense of humor
and have gone out of their way

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:14.987
to cancel everybody from
the past and, you know,

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:17.590
go after every show
that we used to love

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.926
or that "Friends" now, you
know, Jennifer Aniston says,

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:21.928 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Friends" they could
never put on air,

00:09:21.928 --> 00:09:24.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
for the record, I'm more
of a "Seinfeld" guy.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.632
But it just seems like
the obvious extension

00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:28.067
of this hysteria, doesn't it?

00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:31.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, and see what
I do in my book

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:33.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
is rather than say,
okay, all of these things

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:36.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
that people have been canceled
for joking about you can,

00:09:36.609 --> 00:09:39.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
I take it 9 million
steps further.

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I say, there's
not such thing

00:09:41.347 --> 00:09:43.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
as something that
you can't joke about.

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:45.184
And yes, I'm including religion.

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:47.119
Yes, I'm including death.

00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:50.690
Yes, I'm including
terminal illness

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.426
because there's research
that backs this stuff up.

00:09:53.426 --> 00:09:55.194
I'm not just saying this,

00:09:55.194 --> 00:10:00.199 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I write a chapter
obviously about the shitbag,

00:10:01.467 --> 00:10:03.603
which I had that
emergency surgery,

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.638
I had an ostomy for five weeks.

00:10:05.638 --> 00:10:07.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
And as I was being
wheeled into my surgery,

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.809
I was like, what am
I gonna tell people?

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:12.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I know that
everybody's gonna be like,

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:14.714
oh, like so weird
around me about it

00:10:14.714 --> 00:10:16.315
or scared about what to say.

00:10:16.315 --> 00:10:19.352 align:start size:88% position:13%
Same way as when my
mom died at a young age

00:10:19.352 --> 00:10:21.787
and the friends I
wanted to talk to

00:10:21.787 --> 00:10:23.823 align:start size:97% position:3%
weren't the ones with the
most carefully chosen words,

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:26.092
they were the friends
who just were able

00:10:26.092 --> 00:10:27.893
to joke around with me about it.

00:10:29.362 --> 00:10:31.230
I joke around about, I threw
a cats out of the bag party

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.266
after where I had some unused
but already open ostomy bags.

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:36.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were bonging
champagne out of 'em.

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:38.437 align:start size:81% position:19%
And then I started
thinking more,

00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:40.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I had a lot of time
to do nothing but think

00:10:40.172 --> 00:10:41.741
because I had some, you know,

00:10:41.741 --> 00:10:44.110
the ostomy and it was
also the pandemic.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.578
But think about all the people

00:10:45.578 --> 00:10:47.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
who have died
recently of cancers

00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:48.914
we didn't know that they had.

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:50.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like Chadwick Boseman,
like Norm McDonald,

00:10:50.516 --> 00:10:53.119
because they didn't wanna
be treated differently.

00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:55.287 align:start size:97% position:3%
They didn't want the illness
to be the defining thing

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:57.356
that people thought about
when you talked to them.

00:10:57.356 --> 00:11:00.426 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you actually, I mean I
cite this research in my book.

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:01.794
There's people that
are terminally ill,

00:11:01.794 --> 00:11:04.530 align:start size:88% position:13%
people that are, they
treat the terminally ill

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.899
who rank humor as being
super high on their list.

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:09.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even in one study
that was pretty small

00:11:09.702 --> 00:11:13.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
but still higher than
the management of
their physical pain.

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:17.643
When I wrote the chapter,
I Googled searched cancer

00:11:17.643 --> 00:11:19.745
and then how to tell
people I have cancer.

00:11:19.745 --> 00:11:22.214
And the second one
had way more results.

00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:26.419
So clearly everybody
kind of gets this,

00:11:26.419 --> 00:11:28.921 align:start size:81% position:19%
but people are
too afraid because

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:30.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
of what the
consequences might be

00:11:30.623 --> 00:11:32.324
if they say the wrong thing.

00:11:32.324 --> 00:11:35.428 align:start size:88% position:13%
But actually what
you're really doing is,

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:37.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people you're hurting
the most are the ones

00:11:37.596 --> 00:11:40.466
who the rules are supposed
to be in place to protect.

00:11:42.568 --> 00:11:44.103
- Do you sense that's
changing a little bit.

00:11:44.103 --> 00:11:45.404
I mean, even if you
were just looking

00:11:45.404 --> 00:11:46.872
at the success of "Gutfeld!"

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:50.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, it is by far the
number one show in late night,

00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:53.179
which as you know,
on the debut episode,

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:55.047
I predicted that he
would be number one.

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:56.549
I said it was gonna take a year,

00:11:56.549 --> 00:11:58.384
it took him only about
four months or something,

00:11:58.384 --> 00:11:59.919
which is incredible.

00:11:59.919 --> 00:12:02.388 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I mean, the fact is the
one politically incorrect show

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:04.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
on late night is the number
one show in late night.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that shows you that
something's shifting at least.

00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:09.295
Or maybe it's just exclusive to,

00:12:09.295 --> 00:12:11.864
you know, kind of our
side of the aisle.

00:12:11.864 --> 00:12:13.332
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:13.332 --> 00:12:15.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
Especially because there's
so much to joke about

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:18.904
when it comes to, you know,
the current administration

00:12:18.904 --> 00:12:21.307 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think a lot of
other shows are kind of,

00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:22.775
they don't wanna do that.

00:12:23.976 --> 00:12:25.911
I mean, our show, you
can kind of say stuff

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:29.515
that you can definitely not
say on other, on other shows.

00:12:29.515 --> 00:12:30.716
It's a very comfortable place.

00:12:30.716 --> 00:12:32.017
And even for somebody like me,

00:12:32.017 --> 00:12:33.753
where I'm not a Republican,

00:12:33.753 --> 00:12:36.155
I'm Libertarian very much so,

00:12:36.155 --> 00:12:38.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
and everybody who watches
the show knows that,

00:12:38.624 --> 00:12:40.726
I don't fit into
a particular box.

00:12:40.726 --> 00:12:43.562 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I feel very comfortable
on that show expressing myself

00:12:43.562 --> 00:12:46.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
even when I do have
opinions that might differ

00:12:46.365 --> 00:12:50.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
with the, you know, typical
conservative audience.

00:12:50.803 --> 00:12:52.471 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it's just,
everything's cool.

00:12:52.471 --> 00:12:55.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just think that
when we talk about speech

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:58.144
and we talk about comedy,
a lot of the argument is,

00:12:58.144 --> 00:12:59.979
okay, I can say whatever I want

00:12:59.979 --> 00:13:02.815 align:start position:0%
and you can't stop me no matter
how divisive it might be.

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:06.652
And that's true, but that
also I think really ignores

00:13:06.652 --> 00:13:09.922 align:start size:84% position:16%
the way that comedy
and open communication

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:11.791
can bring us all together

00:13:11.791 --> 00:13:14.727
because we're never gonna be
able to understand each other

00:13:14.727 --> 00:13:17.997
if we can't fully express
ourselves to each other.

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:19.498
- Yeah, it's true.

00:13:19.498 --> 00:13:21.167
Do you remember when you first
wanted to make people laugh?

00:13:21.167 --> 00:13:23.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I remember the
exact moment, 1983,

00:13:23.502 --> 00:13:25.204
I was sitting in my
parents' living room

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:26.939
watching Bill Cosby himself.

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:28.908
And this was way
before, you know,

00:13:28.908 --> 00:13:30.342
serial rapist Bill Cosby,

00:13:30.342 --> 00:13:31.844
or at least that we knew of.

00:13:31.844 --> 00:13:33.546 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I remember thinking,
this is, you know,

00:13:33.546 --> 00:13:34.580
the chocolate cake thing.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:35.815
And I was just like,

00:13:35.815 --> 00:13:37.249
this is the funniest
thing I've ever seen.

00:13:37.249 --> 00:13:40.286
That's what I, how could
you not wanna do that?

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:42.555 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, I mean, I've
always been a performer,

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:45.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I have an atrocious
singing voice.

00:13:45.624 --> 00:13:47.893 align:start size:81% position:19%
But I, like, I
try to get lessons

00:13:47.893 --> 00:13:49.428
and the woman called
my mother and said,

00:13:49.428 --> 00:13:51.297
"I don't feel right about
taking your money anymore.

00:13:51.297 --> 00:13:52.731
She's never gonna get it."

00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:54.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I would still audition
for all the musicals

00:13:54.767 --> 00:13:56.936 align:start size:97% position:3%
at the community theater and
I would just get on stage

00:13:56.936 --> 00:13:58.437
and I would sing Happy Birthday.

00:13:58.437 --> 00:14:00.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
Everyone had, you know, vocal
coach and all this stuff,

00:14:00.472 --> 00:14:01.674
and they had these
prepared numbers

00:14:01.674 --> 00:14:03.776
and I'd look at them
like, I'm a star, baby.

00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:05.044
Like, I don't know
what you're thinking.

00:14:05.044 --> 00:14:06.378
Like, put me on the show.

00:14:06.378 --> 00:14:08.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I would always
have these small roles

00:14:08.614 --> 00:14:10.983 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I actually
really truly can't sing.

00:14:10.983 --> 00:14:13.619
So I got more into
comedy and you know,

00:14:13.619 --> 00:14:16.488
it's also something that I
was bullied a lot as a child

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:19.225
and if I could laugh at it,
it became so much easier.

00:14:19.225 --> 00:14:21.460
I mean, even to this day,

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.530
I write a whole chapter
about toxic positivity

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:27.900
where there's this
idea now that if,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.002
you know, if you don't like
something about your body

00:14:30.002 --> 00:14:33.305
or your face or something
like that, just love it.

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:34.573
Instead of not liking it,

00:14:34.573 --> 00:14:36.709
just love it and then
you'll be super happy.

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:39.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I don't doubt that
that would be true,

00:14:39.178 --> 00:14:40.412
but what if you can't?

00:14:40.412 --> 00:14:42.314
I don't think it's
possible for anybody

00:14:42.314 --> 00:14:46.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
to be completely optimistic
about themselves all the time.

00:14:46.085 --> 00:14:48.487
And studies back that up too.

00:14:48.487 --> 00:14:49.989
I mean, this idea
of body positivity

00:14:49.989 --> 00:14:51.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
has been around forever,
but it's actually,

00:14:51.724 --> 00:14:54.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
nobody's actually
capable of doing it.

00:14:54.126 --> 00:14:56.095
And what I found as
a great replacement

00:14:56.095 --> 00:14:59.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
is to make fun of the things
that I hate about myself.

00:14:59.298 --> 00:15:01.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
So if somebody says something
to me about something,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.936
you know, like I
have a flat chest.

00:15:03.936 --> 00:15:05.371 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like, oh, I was already
at brunch joking

00:15:05.371 --> 00:15:07.640
about how my bra size
is double mastectomy.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.608
Like I've already, I beat you

00:15:09.608 --> 00:15:11.377
and I've made these
things less scary

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:13.846
by finding them,
you know, funny.

00:15:13.846 --> 00:15:15.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know, speaking
of health stuff,

00:15:15.047 --> 00:15:16.448
we actually have
something in common

00:15:16.448 --> 00:15:18.851
that I did not know till
you told me privately.

00:15:18.851 --> 00:15:20.052
I'm actually not sure if you've

00:15:20.052 --> 00:15:21.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
talked about it
that much publicly,

00:15:21.487 --> 00:15:24.490 align:start size:97% position:3%
but my audience knows about
seven years ago or so,

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:26.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I first started
getting popular

00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:27.559
and I was getting all this hate

00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:30.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I didn't realize I wasn't
dealing with stress well.

00:15:30.062 --> 00:15:31.897
I developed alopecia

00:15:31.897 --> 00:15:34.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I lost probably
40% of my hair.

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:35.634
Huge chunks all over my head.

00:15:35.634 --> 00:15:38.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went on crazy
experimental medications.

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:40.205
It was by far the most
depressed I've ever been.

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:41.440
I almost quit.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:42.875
Like, I actually had a
night where I was like,

00:15:42.875 --> 00:15:44.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not gonna be on
camera anymore, I'm done.

00:15:44.443 --> 00:15:45.711
I'll just go do something else.

00:15:45.711 --> 00:15:48.847
But you got that too,
not not too long ago,

00:15:48.847 --> 00:15:50.082
but I have to say,
your hair's looking

00:15:50.082 --> 00:15:52.718
quite luxurious at the moment.

00:15:52.718 --> 00:15:54.153
- So I have extensions in,

00:15:54.153 --> 00:15:56.789 align:start size:97% position:3%
but my natural hair is
even like down to here now.

00:15:56.789 --> 00:15:58.023
- Nice. Nice.

00:15:58.023 --> 00:16:00.426
- And at my, you
know, mid twenties

00:16:00.426 --> 00:16:02.761
I lost about a similar amount.

00:16:02.761 --> 00:16:05.497 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was so thin, it was really
patchy and only to here

00:16:05.497 --> 00:16:07.766 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it was like,
see-through, it was very bad.

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:11.470 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wouldn't wear it down
ever without extensions in,

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:13.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I would wear
like hats a lot.

00:16:13.572 --> 00:16:14.873 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'd put my hair
up in a messy bun,

00:16:14.873 --> 00:16:18.277
I'd try to like cover
the parts of my skin.

00:16:18.277 --> 00:16:19.812
It was really, really bad.

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:21.347
Which is why actually I felt,

00:16:21.347 --> 00:16:23.682
I never felt more
relevant than when

00:16:23.682 --> 00:16:25.017
Will Smith hit Chris Rock,

00:16:25.017 --> 00:16:27.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I am a
woman with alopecia.

00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:31.323
I have done standup and
I get how painful it is,

00:16:31.323 --> 00:16:33.192
but also you can't hit somebody.

00:16:33.192 --> 00:16:34.326
- Right.

00:16:34.326 --> 00:16:35.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And I wasn't
surprised to see that

00:16:35.761 --> 00:16:37.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
because of the whole
words are violence thing.

00:16:37.663 --> 00:16:39.665
Because if words are violence

00:16:39.665 --> 00:16:43.402 align:start size:97% position:3%
then violence is an acceptable
response to violence.

00:16:43.402 --> 00:16:45.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I will never understand
how this is being seen

00:16:45.971 --> 00:16:49.308
as a progressive value
because it's actually

00:16:49.308 --> 00:16:52.578
only as we become more
modern and civilized

00:16:52.578 --> 00:16:54.947 align:start size:81% position:19%
as human beings
that we say, okay,

00:16:54.947 --> 00:16:56.815
only words to respond to words.

00:16:56.815 --> 00:17:00.452
I mean, you think about it,
for most of human history,

00:17:00.452 --> 00:17:01.987
even, you know, civil war,

00:17:01.987 --> 00:17:03.255 align:start size:88% position:13%
like you said something
bad about my mom,

00:17:03.255 --> 00:17:05.224
now we're gonna have a
shootout in the yard.

00:17:05.224 --> 00:17:06.525
That's what we used to do.

00:17:06.525 --> 00:17:07.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I don't know why
these people are like,

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:09.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
words are violence
and I'm a progressive.

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:11.463 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like, no, you're
behaving like a Neanderthal.

00:17:12.831 --> 00:17:14.566 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think there's such
a difference between

00:17:14.566 --> 00:17:18.237
expressing something that
maybe hurt your feelings

00:17:18.237 --> 00:17:20.205
because you wanna share
that or you wanna share

00:17:20.205 --> 00:17:23.142 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you were hurt and
trying to use hurt feelings

00:17:23.142 --> 00:17:26.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
to place yourself in a
power position over others.

00:17:26.045 --> 00:17:27.446
And the latter is definitely

00:17:27.446 --> 00:17:28.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
what words are
violence is all about.

00:17:28.981 --> 00:17:30.883
Because as soon as
you've said that

00:17:30.883 --> 00:17:32.418 align:start size:84% position:16%
what that other person
did was violent,

00:17:32.418 --> 00:17:35.554
the fact that you're just
keeping it at verbal only

00:17:35.554 --> 00:17:37.289
is you doing them a favor.

00:17:37.289 --> 00:17:39.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, and they don't
think violence is violence

00:17:39.491 --> 00:17:41.060
because they'll tell
you words are violence,

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.261 align:start size:84% position:16%
but then they'll
tell you burning down

00:17:42.261 --> 00:17:43.796
a Pep Boys is nonviolence.

00:17:43.796 --> 00:17:46.432
Well, ask the guy who's in
the bathroom at Pep Boys.

00:17:46.432 --> 00:17:48.534
Let me ask you this 'cause
you said you're a libertarian

00:17:48.534 --> 00:17:51.370
and you're obviously on Fox,
which is mostly conservative.

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:52.671
And I always tell my audience,

00:17:52.671 --> 00:17:55.507
conservative is a very
wide thing right now,

00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:58.343
if a gay married guy with
two kids whose pro-choice

00:17:58.343 --> 00:18:00.712 align:start size:97% position:3%
can be thought of as a
conservative, then okay, fine.

00:18:00.712 --> 00:18:02.381
